  "query_preview": "..\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Uptick in heat and humidity for midweek.\n\nHigh pressure extending from the Great Lakes to Central\nPennsylvania Monday evening will bring us another mainly clear \nnight with fog forming in the deeper valleys of Central and \nNorthern PA very early Tuesday morning and dissipating an hour \nor two after sunrise.\n\nAbundant sunshine, mixed with some flat cumulus and high, thin  \ncirrus clouds will kick off the shortened school and work week \nTuesday, with a small uptick in temperature and humidity.\n\nAs the center of this area high pressure drifts southeast of PA\non Tuesday and Wednesday, return southwesterly flow will usher \nin an uptick and heat and humidity. \n\nThe never ending upper level ridge of high pressure centered \nover the Central US will also begin to nudge into the Mid \nAtlantic as upper troughing over the Northeast retreats and the \namplified upper level pattern begins to flatten out a bit. \n\nHighs on Tuesday will range from the mid 70s to mid 80s and \nWednesday will trend about 5 degrees warmer areawide. Combined \nwith dewpoints back in the mid to upper 60s, heat index values \nmay crest the 90F mark in the south central mountains on \nWednesday. Dry conditions will prevail and we'll continue to \nenjoy a break from very active stretch of severe weather we've \ndealt with this summer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Showers, storms, and gusty winds Wednesday night\ninto Thursday.\n\nBy Wednesday night, a low pressure system will be moving across\nnorthern Ontario with a cold front extending south through the \nGreat Lakes and Ohio Valley region. The pressure gradient \nwill support a notable uptick in synoptic scale winds Wednesday\nafternoon into Thursday. It's been awhile since we've had \nwidespread gusty winds, but we're getting into the time of year \nwhere such a weather pattern becomes more common. \n\nGusty winds will ramp up Wednesday during the day out of the\nsouthwest ahead of an approaching cold front, and continue\nthrough Thursday afternoon shifting to a westerly direction \nafter the front passes. Wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph seem \nrealistic at this point, with the highest readings occurring at\nhigher elevations in the Laurel Highlands.\n\nThe cold front will also bring a chance for showers and storms\nahead of and along the front. The risk for severe weather will\ndepend on the magnitude of a shortwave trough racing through \nthe region and any associated jet dynamics in play. \n\nVertical wind profiles look unidirectional, so the primary \nhazard would be from damaging winds. Timing of the cold front\nand dewpoints largely staying out of the upper 60s to lower\n70s range are the 2 main reasons to think storms will not get\ntoo crazy, unlike recent events. Timing of the cold front has\nbeen similar the last few days.\n\nRain arrives in northwest PA late Wednesday night and will sag\nsouth across the Commonwealth during the day on Thursday.\n\nHigh pressure builds behind the cold front, giving way to a dry\nand sunny Friday. There could be a few isolated PM showers and a\nstorm or two on Saturday or Sunday, but most locations will stay\ndry.",
